# CVE log

Audit findings and what was decided. Current state: `pnpm audit`.
Transitive pins are raised via `pnpm.overrides` in `package.json`.

## 2026-08-23 — Vercel build compatibility and dependency refresh

Updated `next` and `eslint-config-next` from 16.2.12 to 16.3.2. The Vercel
adapter cannot be combined with Next's `output: "standalone"` copy step: it
consumes the trace files during packaging, after which Next's standalone step
can fail reading the server trace. `next.config.ts` now disables standalone
output only when `NEXT_ADAPTER_PATH` or `VERCEL` is set; Docker and local
production builds retain it.

Refined the advisory overrides so they upgrade only compatible major lines:
brace-expansion 1.x and 2.x, js-yaml 3.x and 4.x, and nanoid 3.x. In
particular, brace-expansion 5.x remains untouched because forcing it to 1.x
breaks ESLint's bundled minimatch dependency. Confirmed clean with `pnpm audit`.

## 2026-08-14 — resolved 8 findings

Superseded by the scoped, compatible-major overrides recorded on 2026-08-23.

Resolved via `pnpm.overrides` in `package.json`. All 3 existing brace-expansion versions (1.1.16, 2.1.2, 5.0.8) replaced with 1.1.18 — pnpm applies overrides universally. This is safe because brace-expansion 1.x and 2.x share the same function export API (only 5.x changed to named `{ expand }` exports, which we do NOT use).

| Package      | From → To              | How      | Advisories resolved                                                 |
| ------------ | ---------------------- | -------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| brace-expansion | 1.1.16→1.1.18, 2.1.2→1.1.18, 5.0.8→1.1.18 | override | CVE-2026-14257, CVE-2026-59870 (all versions)                       |
| js-yaml      | 3.15.0→3.15.1, 4.3.0→4.3.1 | override | CVE-2026-59870 (GHSA-5p4m-2wfm-xmqj)                                |
| nanoid       | 3.3.16→3.3.18          | override | CVE-2026-45870 (GHSA-2v37-7h3g-55p8)                                |

Confirmed clean: `pnpm audit` reports 0 vulnerabilities. `pnpm lint`, `pnpm typecheck`, and `pnpm test` all pass.

